you're watching the daily climate show  on sky news on today's programme  plastic waste problem pining up in  turkey we look at what's happening to  the uk's waste  [Applause]  my britons are being asked to get green  fingered in honour of the queen's  tribally  and we find out what it's like to live  and work in the antarctic  studying climate change  [Music]  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show where we track the  changes happening to our world right now  and meet those coming up with the  solutions and coming up as britons are  once again allowed to jet off abroad  i'll be speaking to a sustainable travel  expert  about how to make your getaway more  eco-friendly  but first it's been revealed that  plastic waste exported from the uk to  turkey  is being dumped and burned according to  greenpeace  it's illegal to export plastic waste  from the uk  unless it's set to be recycled or  incinerated in an energy from waste  plant but investigators for the campaign  group  found supermarket packaging and plastic  bags by the roadside in  fields and spilling into waterways in 10  sites around adana province  in southern turkey now it's less than  100 kilometers from turkey's largest  port  a convenient stopping point for  container vessels  we're around 40 percent of the uk's  plastic waste exports were sent to  turkey  last year that's approximately 210  000 tons and it's a huge increase from  just  four years before when around 12 000  tons  were sent to the country the uk produces  more plastic waste per capita than  anywhere else in the world except the us  in 2018 we generated more than 5 million  tons of it that's enough to fill wembley  stadium  six times over but turkey's official  waste recovery rate  is the lowest of any oecd country just  12  in 2018 according to the country's  statistics institution  well the investigation by greenpeace  found waste including packaging from uk  supermarkets dumped across riverbanks by  the roadside  and on the coast and in many cases the  plastic was on fire  or had been burned to try to dispose of  it  with me now is sam chetten welsh  political campaigner at greenpeace  so what can the government do to ensure  that any plastic waste that's sent from  the uk to be recycled  actually is recycled and doesn't end up  burned like this  well thanks anna there's a huge amount  that the government can do actually  there's a very  active role they can play in sorting  this the first thing that they can do  uh you know the government has already  actually made a promise  to have a consultation on banning the  export of plastic waste but at the  moment their consultation  wouldn't include countries like turkey  and obviously that is where  we found so much of this waste turning  up being dumped and burned  but fundamentally the root cause of this  problem is that we are simply making and  using far too much plastic here in the  uk  and the government we believe has an  obligation to set targets so that  retailers reduce the amount of  single-use plastic that they use  and if we reduce single-use plastic by  50 by 2025  we can end the export of plastic waste  so we need to reduce the amount of  plastic waste that we generate but what  about recycling the government's  acknowledged the uk should and wants to  do more  of recycling in this country so what's  got to happen to allow that to increase  sure well i mean a big part of the  problem uh in terms of what we're  finding in those rubbish dumps that  you're looking at now on your screen  is a massive amount of incredibly mixed  plastic waste  that means plastic waste that you know  it is not really recyclable in any  reasonable in any reasonable way unlike  things like drinks bottles which you can  tend to recycle a fair few times but  ultimately recycling can only play a  very small specific role in this problem  because plastic as many people may not  know  is only fully recyclable about two or  three times before you can no longer  really use it  and that's why we're calling on the  government to reduce the amount of  plastic that we're using we have to  start with reduction first  that means switching to reuse and refill  you know  taking things back to the shop to be  refilled and those are the kind of  solutions that we need to see from the  government as  urgently sam chetten welsh thanks very  much indeed  thank you and we did ask the government  about their take on this and a  spokeswoman for the department for  environment food and rural affairs told  sky news  we are clear that the uk should handle  more of its waste at home  and that's why we're committed to  banning the export of plastic waste  to non-oecd countries and clamping down  on illegal waste  exports including to countries such as  turkey  through tougher controls well in today's  other climate news now the uk's  water sector will invest 2.8 billion  pounds in environmental projects  after the watchdog off-what put forward  a plan to lower pollution from utilities  the new projects will include  improvements in drinking water treatment  to reduce energy and chemical usage and  working with local partners to reduce  the risk of flooding  and protecting habitats  [Applause]  coral can evolve to save itself from  climate change according to new research  scientists have found that some corals  can swap out the algae that live  inside their tissue for different  strains that are more tolerant to warmer  temperatures  which could help them survive moderate  climate change  [Applause]  and there's a new big five of the  world's creatures but to shoot with a  camera  rather than shoot to kill it includes  elephants  polar bears gorillas tigers and lions  and was made by the new big five  initiative  that seeks to raise awareness of the  crisis facing the world's wildlife  the old big five the five toughest  animals in africa to hunt  were lions leopards rhinos elephants and  buffalo now the queen is no stranger to  a bit of local climate action having  planted an estimated 1500 trees  during her reign and now people across  the country are being asked to plant a  tree  for the jubilee sky's royal  correspondent rihanna mills reports  the prince has planted a young oak tree  specially flown to canada  from windsor park for decades all around  the world  it's been part of the royal day job the  queen  planting more than 1500 trees during her  reign  so it couldn't feel more fitting that  we're all being asked  to plant a tree for her platinum jubilee  it left me much to do  in windsor in march the prince of wales  watched by his mother  planted the first as patron of the  project  whether you are an individual hoping to  plant a single sapling in your garden  a school or community group planting a  tree everyone across the country  can get involved one of the founding  partners royal mail have planted trees  in every nation to kick-start the  project  but the rest of us are being asked to  wait until the next planting season  starting in october giving schools  villages businesses and  individuals the summer to plan the  woodland trust  giving away 3 million saplings to  support it on top of  expanding tree and woodland cover across  the country this is also a celebration  of ancient trees and ancient woodlands  the queen's green canopy will dedicate a  network of 70  ancient woodlands across the united  kingdom and identify  70 important ancient trees to mark the  jubilee  reanon mills sky news  from today britons are allowed to travel  abroad on a limited basis as coronavirus  restrictions are significantly scaled  back  it's great news for holiday makers and  airlines but it could be damaging for  the climate as global emissions creep  back up to pre-pandemic levels  so is it possible to go on a foreign  holiday and be climate friendly  well juliet kinsman sustainability  editor of the travel magazine conde nast  traveler joins me now welcome to you so  if you do want a  foreign holiday what are your tips to be  as green as possible on that trip  i mean i tend not to use the word  sustainable as much actually but think  about  positive impact so how can we go away  and think that our trip is  actually leaving uh good in the  destinations we visit well we have to  think more about the social and economic  factors  um flying is never going to be good for  the environment we need to make those  emissions  count i will say a little tip around  flying obviously always fly direct  because it's the taking off and the  landing part that  emits the most carbon so if you can just  fly direct that's really  it's really going to help but in terms  of where we go  and who we give our money to that is a  critical way to help particularly after  what we've gone through in the last year  to give you an example  in terms of booking a holiday thinking  about who you're giving your money to  choose independent local businesses  where the money stays in  local communities ideally that might  even be a business that's working  really hard to tackle the challenges  which are  behind the problems causing the the  climate emergency so  perhaps a lodge or a hotel that is  investing and protecting in  biodiversity is rewilding conservation  is at the heart of what they do  by having a holiday there we're  supporting supporting that uh  obviously great preservation but also  as i say the socio-economic side of  things uh if you look at africa  typically if you look at kenya had a  couple million uh visitors in 2019 to  their wildlife reserves  now that will have dropped 80 to 90  percent in 2020 which means  about a million and a half people will  have lost their jobs  that then puts pressure on their  wildlife areas in terms of illegal  logging in terms of  poaching in terms of bush meat pressures  so what we want to do is use that  holiday money  maybe we can't travel tomorrow to kenya  for example it's still on the red list  but we can invest in holidays that are  going to pay it forward  juliet kinsman thanks very much indeed  now in the latest in our series of  climate diaries we're hearing from  scientists from the british antarctic  survey whose work helps us better  understand how climate change is  affecting our planet  they're based at the rotherha research  station on adelaide island to the west  of the antarctic peninsula  where the sun never rises above the  horizon in winter and temperatures can  drop as low as minus 20 degrees  hello my name is nadja frontier  my name is ryan matthews i'm the marine  assistant  at rather research station which is to  the west of the west  antarctic peninsula  so far i've been here for five months  and it is an absolutely wonderful  experience  so ryan how much wildlife have you seen  any whales  we have a resident pod of orca we see  the orca  on occasion once a week  [Music]  i really hope to build a more  comprehensive picture of what is  happening in antarctic ecosystems  over the season and also try and compare  this with past data  so we can untangle the effect of climate  change of what is just happening  naturally in such uh extreme environment  since 1997 we collect data  once a week during the winter periods  and twice a week during the summer  periods  so it's a long term time series to help  measure and understand how our oceans  are changing  there are definitely many many highs  living with your colleagues is a  fantastic experience  when we have finished the day we come  together with all the station that's  everyone that's working here and we  share meal time so it's a really great  communal time  also what's on our doorstep is is the  ramp so this is our local ski area  after a day in the office or a day maybe  if we haven't been out on the boat it's  definitely great  to get outside and and go for a ski so  we're really privileged to be here  well we've heard today how people are  being encouraged to plant a tree to mark  the queen's jubilee but  tomorrow we'll be hearing about a  project on a rather larger scale to  triple the rate of tree planting  across england we'll have all the  details here on the daily climate show  tomorrow see you then  [Music]  you

